<template>
    <SiteShell page="features">
        <div class="page-head">
            <span class="chip chip--accent">In development</span>
            <h1>Features</h1>
            <p class="lead">
                Workbook reading, formula inspection and agent authoring share
                one declared model. Nothing here is assumed from a file
                extension.
            </p>
        </div>

        <section class="section">
            <div class="section-head">
                <h2>Contracts</h2>
                <p>Four commitments that exist in the codebase, independent of the engine.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="cards">
                <article class="card">
                    <h3>Workbook handles</h3>
                    <p>
                        Create, open, save, inspect and export address a
                        workbook by an opaque handle. The client never keeps a
                        second, editable copy of the grid.
                    </p>
                    <span class="chip">declared</span>
                </article>
                <article class="card">
                    <h3>Revisions</h3>
                    <p>
                        Revisions are monotonic. A writer that starts from a
                        stale revision is rejected, so two edits cannot
                        silently overwrite each other.
                    </p>
                    <span class="chip">declared</span>
                </article>
                <article class="card">
                    <h3>Stable error codes</h3>
                    <p>
                        Failures carry a stable code such as
                        <code>capability/not-implemented</code>, not a free-form
                        string. A caller can branch on the code.
                    </p>
                    <span class="chip">declared</span>
                </article>
                <article class="card">
                    <h3>Capability matrix</h3>
                    <p>
                        Import, edit, export and inspect are reported per
                        format, alongside checks, renderers and export targets.
                    </p>
                    <span class="chip">available</span>
                </article>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="section">
            <div class="section-head">
                <h2>Feature areas</h2>
                <p>Each area names its real state in this build.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="areas">
                <article class="area">
                    <div class="area__head">
                        <h3>Workbooks</h3>
                        <span class="chip">declared</span>
                    </div>
                    <p>
                        A workbook project carries stable sheet ids,
                        order-only indexes, sheet names and cell counts. The
                        host returns the workbook shape; the studio does not
                        synthesize one.
                    </p>
                </article>
                <article class="area">
                    <div class="area__head">
                        <h3>Formula inspection</h3>
                        <span class="chip">declared</span>
                    </div>
                    <p>
                        Six checks are declared: formulas, references, cycles,
                        types, ranges and styles. Every finding carries a sheet
                        id and an A1-style address, and a report states whether
                        it passed.
                    </p>
                </article>
                <article class="area">
                    <div class="area__head">
                        <h3>Export and delivery</h3>
                        <span class="chip">declared</span>
                    </div>
                    <p>
                        Declared targets are <code>xlsx</code>,
                        <code>xlsm</code>, <code>csv</code>, <code>json</code>
                        and <code>pdf</code>. A delivery report lists what the
                        target kept and what it lost. PDF is a projection and
                        is reported as not editable on reopen.
                    </p>
                </article>
                <article class="area">
                    <div class="area__head">
                        <h3>Studio shell</h3>
                        <span class="chip">shell only</span>
                    </div>
                    <p>
                        Titlebar, path bar, sheet rail, rulers, inspector and
                        diagnostics exist. The cell renderer does not, so no
                        values are painted and no workbook is fabricated.
                    </p>
                </article>
                <article class="area">
                    <div class="area__head">
                        <h3>Skills</h3>
                        <span class="chip">declared</span>
                    </div>
                    <p>
                        A packaged task guide for agents. When an operation is
                        unavailable it reports
                        <code>capability/not-implemented</code> instead of
                        routing around the contract with local ZIP or XML work.
                    </p>
                </article>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="section">
            <div class="section-head">
                <h2>Command surface</h2>
                <p>What each surface can do today, without rounding up.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="table-wrap">
                <table class="table">
                    <thead>
